data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Tested on .NET 11 (preview) and it works perfectly:

```csharp
Span<nint> nints = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

Both compiled and ran without issues. The native-sized integers (`nint`/`nuint`, introduced in C# 9) are valid unmanaged types, so they satisfy the `stackalloc` constraint. On this 64-bit system, `nuint.MaxValue` printed `18446744073709551615` (2⁶⁴−1) and `IntPtr.Size` was 8, confirming 8-byte native integers.
